default. Each batch takes every object of that environment to the global
environment of its worker. A data function and an action function then find \code{CFG} and
the other functions of the folder. A plnr plan and \code{.Random.seed} are left
out. Each batch takes a copy of each object, so keep large objects out of
that environment.

\code{run_plan()} does not copy the R options, for example
\code{options(digits = 3)}, and no other state of this R session. A future sets
the option \code{mc.cores} to 1. \code{run_plan()} removes it on a worker, so
\code{\link[parallel:mclapply]{parallel::mclapply()}} in an analysis uses its default number of cores.
The output, the messages and the warnings of a worker come to this R
session at the end of each batch.

After the run, \code{run_plan()} stops the cluster and registers the sequential
foreach backend, also after an error. It sets the future plan and the
option \code{doFuture.rng.onMisuse} back to their values before the run. Each
data function and each analysis sets its own seed, so \code{run_plan()} turns
off the warning of doFuture about random numbers for the run.
plnr calls each data function once for each scenario and simulation, see
\code{\link[=add_data]{add_data()}}. Each data function and each analysis sets its own seed
before it runs, see \code{\link[=add_analyses]{add_analyses()}}. Take a function that depends only on
its arguments, the objects of the functions folder such as \code{CFG}, the
attached packages and the seed. Its results with \code{workers = 1} and with
\code{workers = 2} are \code{identical()}. A function that reads an R option or another state
of the main R session can give other results on a worker. A
data.table loses its self-reference on the way back from a worker, and
\code{run_plan()} gives it a new one with \code{\link[data.table:setalloccol]{data.table::setalloccol()}}. It does
this for each data.table in a value or in a plain list in a value, at any
depth. It does not do this for a data.table in a list column of a
data.frame. Such a value MAY differ in \code{identical()} between 1 worker and
more.

An error in an action function fails that analysis, and an error in a
data function fails each analysis that gets the data. pbn saves no results
of a run with a failed analysis, so the first failed analysis stops the
run. The section Stop at the first failure gives the rules. After the run,
\code{run_plan()} prints each failed analysis with its name and its full error
message with \code{cat()}, and then it stops. R cuts an error message at 1000
bytes by default, and \code{cat()} does not. The error message of \code{run_plan()}
gives the number of failed analyses and of the analyses that did not run.
It also names the first failed analysis in the order of the plan, and
holds its error. pbn cuts that error to keep the message below 900 bytes.
When 2 or more analyses fail and all have the same error, the message says
so. The results files of an earlier run stay as they are. After
\code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}}, \code{run_plan()} writes the run log
\verb{<version>_results/run.md} with the failures before it stops. The section
The run log of \code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}} describes it.

After \code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}}, \code{run_plan()} first replaces the run log with
one that says that the run has not finished, and deletes the errors file
\verb{<version>_results/run_errors_gitexclude.md}. A call of \code{run_plan()}
without \code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}} after it leaves this log.

\code{run_plan()} measures the elapsed time of each data step and of each
analysis where it runs, also on a worker, and the time of the clock of the
computer at its end. The times go to the record of the plan, and
\code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}} writes them to the run log. They are not in the value, so
two runs stay \code{identical()}.

The value carries the attribute \code{pbn_run}, a stamp of the plan. It holds
no count of the runs, so two runs of one plan can be \code{identical()}.
\code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}} stops on a value without the stamp of its plan.

\code{run_plan()} sets \code{use_foreach} of the plan: \code{FALSE} for 1 worker and
\code{TRUE} for more.

After \code{\link[=initialize_project]{initialize_project()}}, \code{run_plan()} first lists the files of the
folders of the project again, with their sizes and modification times.
It reads no content of a file. The value carries this fingerprint in its
stamp, and \code{\link[=save_results]{save_results()}} writes it to \code{manifest$data_files}.

An analysis that changes the shared data in place fails, also for a change
of values such as \code{data$d[1, x := 0]}. The error names the data and each
changed column. An analysis that changes an object of the functions
folder in place fails too. So does an analysis that leaves a new object in
